      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "During the Edo period, Hirakata thrived as a bustling post station and river port linking Kyoto to Osaka.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/place/Hirakata",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Kitamura Miso, a 100‑year‑old brewery, still crafts miso by hand and offers salt‑koji tastings to visitors.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://osaka-info.jp/en/special/deep-hirakata/",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Cyclists can ride electric bikes through the historic roads of Hirakata‑shuku, the old post town that once hosted travelers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://osaka-info.jp/en/special/deep-hirakata/",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Strolling along the Yodo River or hiking the nearby Ikoma mountains lets visitors experience Hirakata’s blend of riverine and mountainous scenery.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
